               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="11XX/12XX" notBefore="1100" notAfter="1299"
                        calendar="#gregorian">s. xii--xiii</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_219">Fountains, Yorkshire.
                        Cistercian abbey of St Mary the Virgin</orgName><p><q>Liber monasterii de
                           Fontibus. Theca [...] nov. arm. lib. xvi. 16: in propria classe codicem
                           post lecta seconde ...</q>.</p><q type="pressmark">Th.[ ] in nov.
                        lib.16</q></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Sir H.
                        Ingilby</persName> of Ripley Castle (cf. Hist. MSS. Comm. Rep. 6. 356a).
                     Sold 21 October 1920 (Sotheby lot 116) to Quaritch (£18).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Owned by <persName role="fmo">Henry
                        Vyner</persName> of Studley Royal and on loan to the Archives Department of
                     Leeds Public Libraries (referenced there VR 6107). Vyner was listed as the
                     current owner of this book -- along with four others from Fountains Abbey -- in
                     the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Purchased by the British Museum on 19
                     November 1981 from Hofmann and Freeman, along with three of the other Fountains
                     books previously owned by Vyner. Compare Additional MSS <ref
                        target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_2883">62130</ref>, <ref
                        target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_2881">62131</ref> and
                        <ref target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_2885">62132A</ref>.
                     Note also that fragments of a missal formerly with Additional MS 62129 were
                     identified as pastedowns from Additional MS 62132A and are now Additional MS
                        <ref target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_9747">62132B</ref>.
                     These British Library shelfmarks were reported in the supplement to Medieval
                     Libraries of Great Britain (1987), but at that point the remaining Vyner book
                     was untraced. It is now known to be <ref
                        target="https://mlgb.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/catalog/book_2884">New Haven (CT),
                        Beinecke Library, MS Osborn fa42</ref>.</provenance>
               </history>
